  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Tuesday) \nIssued at 1222 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nWidespread showers along with chances for thunderstorms are\nexpected to be ongoing Thursday morning over eastern Oklahoma and\nnorthwest Arkansas while the area of low pressure makes its way\ninto western Oklahoma/west central Kansas. The main convective\narea is currently forecast to push east and southeast Thursday\ninto Thursday night with the highest PoPs across southeast \nOklahoma and western Arkansas by Thursday evening. A continued\nlimited severe potential as well as an ongoing heavy rain threat\nremain forecast Thursday over much of the region. Like\nWednesday/Wednesday night, overall instability is forecast to be\nmarginal. \n\nBehind the main area of showers/storms, scattered lighter rain\nshowers become possible for northeast Oklahoma by Thursday evening\nand continues into Friday. This is in response to a mid level \nlayer of drier air wrapping into the low pressure system as it\nbecomes more elongated from southwest to northeast and lifts off \nto the northeast. Thus, the heavy rain threat is forecast to \nreside over southeast Oklahoma into western Arkansas Thursday \nnight into Friday morning.\n\nAlso Thursday night into Friday, an upper level trof axis is\nprogged to move eastward across the northern Plains, and push a\ntrailing cold front through the Plains into the CWA. This will \nalso help to push the greater shower and limited storm potential \nsoutheast through the region Friday. By Friday afternoon, the \nhigher PoPs look to move out of the CWA with the movement of the \ncold front. Behind the front, scattered rain chances and limited \nthunder potential continues Friday afternoon/evening for the CWA \nwhile a portion of the low pressure systems trof axis holds back \nover the region. Low rain chances look to linger into Saturday \nnight before the weak trof axis gets a push eastward from a second\nupper level trof moving across the Plains. Behind the second \nwave, drier conditions are forecast for next week. \n\nWhen all said and done, widespread rainfall amounts of 2 to 3.5\ninches with locally higher amounts approaching 5 inches are\nforecast Wednesday evening through Friday night. The majority of\nthis rainfall is current forecast Wednesday night through Friday\nmorning, which again could create localized flooding conditions.\nThe greater potential is over east central and southeast Oklahoma. \n\nLastly, temperatures after Wednesday are forecast to become more\nfall like with high temperatures Friday in the 70s to low 80s. The\ncooler conditions are forecast to remain for the weekend into\nearly next week as well.",
